  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nNorthern stream midlevel flow remains situated over the Great Lakes \ntoday with a moderate jet core of over 110 knots. An embedded \nshortwave currently positioned north of Lake Superior continues to \ndig this afternoon and make its way across the U.S./Canadian border \nthis afternoon with enough energy to extend some moisture into the \nnorthern parts of the CWA this evening. Otherwise, conditions remain \nmostly cloudy and mild today and Tuesday.\n\nAnother trough develops over the upper Midwest early Wednesday \nmorning and deepens over the northeastern U.S. by the end of the \nwork week. Surface low pressure associated with the trough will dig \nacross the Ohio Valley while deepening. Synoptic-driven \nprecipitation will commence this Wednesday followed by lake effect \naround the Thursday/Friday timeframe. The most noticeable weather \nchange will be the temperature change as cold cP air becomes drawn \ninto the northwoods. Current highs in the 40s and 50s will be \nreplaced by upper 30s to low 40s. Long-range guidance depicts more \nstable conditions this weekend as upstream ridging begins to build, \nreturning warmer temperatures along with midlevel subsidence as we \nhead into late November. \n\n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday and Tuesday: Pretty benign conditions continue today as cloud \ncover slowly deteriorates and stable air builds from the southwest. \nCurrent radar depicts some light showers pivoting across Ontario \nwhich are attempting to stretch into the eastern Upper Peninsula. \nModel soundings show saturated profiles of low to moderate column \ndepth (less than 2000ft) which could cause one or two locations to \nobserve a trace of rainfall this evening, but most areas are \nexpected to remain dry. Surface high pressure to our southwest will \ncontinue to briefly dominate the region as Southwest winds overnight \nkeep lows in the 40s before turning WNW this Tuesday. Expect \nwidespread highs in the 50s Tuesday with breezy winds gusting to the \n20s.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: The aforementioned cyclone development will \nbe positioned over Central Indian this Wednesday. Unorganized \nstratiform rainfall will be observed this Wednesday with amounts of \na tenth inch or less. Some snow gets introduced into the forecast \novernight Wednesday as the surface cyclone progresses southeast and \ncold air begins to be drawn in from the north. Precipitation type \nand coverage switches to banded lake effect, impacting areas \ntypically affected by northerly flow. Profiles are still too warm to \nproduce effective snow growth as the majority of saturation \ntemperatures remain above -10 Celsius. With warm soil temperatures \nand highs remaining above freezing this Thursday, isolated areas \nthat could see accumulations under bands are likely to see only a \ntenth or so of accumulations. \n\nFriday through Sunday Outlook: Longwave midlevel troughing quickly \ngets replaced by ridging this weekend. Northern stream flow with \ncold cP air will become cut off to the north once again as highs \nprogressively increase this weekend, reaching the 50s by Sunday. \nSubsidence aloft will also support surface high pressure to build \nthis weekend. Little to no precipitation is expected through the \nremainder of the forecast period as long-range ensembles favor \nmild conditions as we head into next week.",
